[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9a2db1-529d-5628-9061-7052c51aa8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447347} {:answer/id #uuid "cc49cf9f-93a9-5729-904e-6ab3eebbf63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447352} {:answer/id #uuid "cf37dabf-a5f5-5488-94e7-124530583cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447353} {:answer/id #uuid "f66bf744-8b15-5c45-869d-f7226d3e3a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447360} {:answer/id #uuid "fa96e6a2-688c-5137-81fa-3a6eabc7070a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc49cf9f-93a9-5729-904e-6ab3eebbf63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447352} :answer-field/id #uuid "16678482-e75d-56bd-a681-d31f80ad72e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "caca4712-f17f-5ed4-8f57-6c6c5c27d1ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-130213" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $x{y}^{2}=3-2y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{y}^{2}+2y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2xy+2 & =0 \\\\ xy+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{(-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2} & =3-2(-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =3+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get $y=3,$ and the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{3},3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624f56b5-cbb2-5d69-80aa-3a89f3efdffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447527} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447535} {:answer/id #uuid "94453db4-5163-5f30-85de-172a8762bebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3},2,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447558} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe475a3-f48f-577e-a802-8ae141564162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3},3,\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447575} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b12dda-2d3e-5bf9-9783-6d0e1a0f404f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{7}{4},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1b51cc-2c9d-583b-ba44-ab011436c6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c161c5dd-50c8-5d52-9605-51baf6787143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "068c9cd0-bc02-5de8-abd9-1e9171a4647c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106255" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,4)-f(1,1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,4⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩) \\\\ 0-(-4) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4y,4x,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{1}-8{c}_{3} \\\\ 1 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=3t+1,\\,z=2t+2,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=3t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=2t+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)+(3t+1)-2(2t+2) & =1 \\\\ 3t+3+3t+1-4t-4 & =1 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009695df-eb16-5bf9-8f7d-15e791cfc73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447477} {:answer/id #uuid "2849b4f1-2032-5e2d-8fa6-aa8714e84738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447496} {:answer/id #uuid "3425035f-0d1e-509a-81a3-b03031eebdd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447501} {:answer/id #uuid "490c666d-eddd-5d4c-ab55-df222ee5f8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447510} {:answer/id #uuid "7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af439be-5db7-5983-911f-bb80da9f317f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447541} :answer-field/id #uuid "88af047f-5cd6-548d-8819-c007bc77243d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ee35382-ba28-5748-9984-ae6f2ae68bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-106260" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5363ad4a-c20f-5eee-ab84-c143d8ef952f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447515} {:answer/id #uuid "62463d21-cbcf-5ef9-ad10-6911765c493d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{8}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447526} {:answer/id #uuid "75015542-06ee-5674-9545-922c88590d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447538} {:answer/id #uuid "8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447553} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d8bb3-e805-5778-91b8-30c1d0b219f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{8}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d990879-07c0-5de8-b720-dbde041af8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447553} :answer-field/id #uuid "c63b3c1b-d356-5e8b-ac3a-3b6933382aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be46c0d6-9d63-5ba9-b07d-0c281a7d7cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130903" :question/problem "Determine the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ -1-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2}-y,-x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-(2{c}_{1}+1)-2{c}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ 3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-4{c}_{1}+1 & =0 \\\\ (3{c}_{1}-1)({c}_{1}-1) & =0 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(\\frac{1}{3})+1=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,3).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102ed920-ce8d-56fe-b777-875c7526e3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447486} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447522} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9cb7a3-ee66-57e2-b4d2-ed1892ce5fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447523} {:answer/id #uuid "6aaf3b28-e8a1-5430-9157-532cf5a5a3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447533} {:answer/id #uuid "7e59e230-39cc-5cb0-843b-3ae813f2f120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd61385-d886-5f5b-99fd-e976df1a87a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447522} :answer-field/id #uuid "e986ee90-2a8a-564a-b524-a14859996d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db61b5b3-00ff-5d08-ac35-b46068ec6075" :question/math-academy-id "q-106860" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb0f1f8-2c8c-587d-af87-78c647b1cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454969} {:answer/id #uuid "52950150-cf38-5785-b5c4-0a95b436b499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455050} {:answer/id #uuid "84793b57-cdb0-527c-8f47-4fc489aedbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455140} {:answer/id #uuid "928594cf-1e82-52f9-95c7-79551dbc7ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455169} {:answer/id #uuid "adebc696-6cad-5a00-9edf-015f1843d18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84793b57-cdb0-527c-8f47-4fc489aedbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455140} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a3ae46-0e2c-5017-bfca-74625ceef8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b9e5c87-171d-5626-ae59-fa7b69d9a95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76766" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}-2e \\\\  & =2e({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c0f1a3a-2816-584e-a58f-1a944b890014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455004} {:answer/id #uuid "59542311-3beb-5ba9-afe8-6ee4234a603f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455059} {:answer/id #uuid "65217f58-febb-5f88-af93-f698f3e104ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455086} {:answer/id #uuid "aac6174e-2cde-5d16-8869-626f99dc3227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455201} {:answer/id #uuid "d5463613-7aa5-5ac0-8e87-c4d1223952c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5463613-7aa5-5ac0-8e87-c4d1223952c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455273} :answer-field/id #uuid "157407e8-4b6e-5f9e-8bc5-3a22df95510b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22016566-6911-5c05-b305-606b27ad2ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76584" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239cca6c-804d-568c-9236-141e4ca7f897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454956} {:answer/id #uuid "2e30646b-950b-5207-8b78-2d3832e125e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454973} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5d6c92-e2a1-55af-af72-56edfe9fbc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455042} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbb07ca-e7d7-58c3-8c34-f10c0c9661fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455130} {:answer/id #uuid "94132ebc-7150-5f36-9359-19697117b692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e5d6c92-e2a1-55af-af72-56edfe9fbc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455042} :answer-field/id #uuid "015c6706-f693-57f9-9ba0-4544584eb2cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ed7acf8-9750-5997-81ee-fb6a8cd2e910" :question/math-academy-id "q-76685" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "404520d3-d967-5b0b-9a92-89f881bf696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455012} {:answer/id #uuid "890fe5ec-8858-5534-8b26-e75adee4e582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455150} {:answer/id #uuid "90adc149-a9e2-5b9c-8313-fbc0425f524a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455166} {:answer/id #uuid "e503b46f-e7bf-57d7-8559-2e0601cdad27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455298} {:answer/id #uuid "ef25c8f6-c622-5edf-b418-bcdc116be329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "404520d3-d967-5b0b-9a92-89f881bf696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455012} :answer-field/id #uuid "63750d47-51f3-5cdf-a76b-72522571295c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f1c8267-786a-5d55-8970-d1a062c41c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76673" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a10cbc-62bc-52ef-9573-9bb8535117b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454915} {:answer/id #uuid "2e04cc6e-105d-58a6-b535-adb2aa80e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454971} {:answer/id #uuid "5374193f-6c53-5136-b249-4387a6f7f018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455054} {:answer/id #uuid "9a399ff3-ac04-580d-8f27-2c4ed2b64de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455184} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4fc49c-efc5-5ad5-9f98-7f2015d47df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a10cbc-62bc-52ef-9573-9bb8535117b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454915} :answer-field/id #uuid "98645239-f570-5bdc-ae35-ee32a0ef62bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50247cc3-8637-5b67-95c3-4cdd3dd3329e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76582" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b297024-5803-5e1f-806e-e9147fe90127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455031} {:answer/id #uuid "77014ac8-9188-566a-ab76-fd0f1159cb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455121} {:answer/id #uuid "a33f9d01-31ec-5113-88f0-e574bdf8e345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455192} {:answer/id #uuid "cf548da9-86b9-58e8-bfb8-e84b31a042ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455267} {:answer/id #uuid "f822b4a7-0b96-5267-a032-717ecb4736cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b297024-5803-5e1f-806e-e9147fe90127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455031} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa59a1c-e79f-5e55-9bec-480e8a735e4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50bd506e-5f91-5da4-a6e7-565dbeb70122" :question/math-academy-id "q-165416" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417e9b86-46f1-5505-b2b8-827b75bf916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455013} {:answer/id #uuid "467d0a11-3d59-5056-8c67-302264331708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455021} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a330-e973-52eb-b27c-39dd3a1bb1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455038} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcb936a-b5c3-5867-a96a-a7f463d31cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a330-e973-52eb-b27c-39dd3a1bb1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455038} :answer-field/id #uuid "8703dd91-2500-56b8-9fc9-0a71b2701f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58703cdd-6a5b-5d3e-93ac-66e311d20a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344278" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $8$-periodic on $[-4,4]$, we have $L=4$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f635389-edc5-5dc3-8b76-ade3a6532e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454953} {:answer/id #uuid "33d20bdd-920e-5017-9ff8-f223643bc0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454984} {:answer/id #uuid "6e341854-6e46-5393-acbf-d555137a5f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455107} {:answer/id #uuid "b3348cc7-e699-5af8-bc08-746cd7a662ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455214} {:answer/id #uuid "dffb9715-18d6-51d1-8e5a-8408e61f22c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d20bdd-920e-5017-9ff8-f223643bc0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454984} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad46ea88-b115-5458-b648-47a63580c1e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f02772c-72fa-5f47-bb75-202fca199929" :question/math-academy-id "q-35273" :question/problem "If \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\text{rank}(F).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $F,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 13 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(F)=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a88e0b9-efe3-5c60-850b-a590ab5c7a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454927} {:answer/id #uuid "585c7fa1-6f4c-5c7c-9aa3-76aa043504d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455057} {:answer/id #uuid "5a95b8e9-6493-5f50-a29f-7ee9d41b4718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455061} {:answer/id #uuid "a7330c13-ecf9-5fb5-8378-1f535c4f0420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455199} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4e334f-cfe8-5326-92bb-f4acab21965f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7330c13-ecf9-5fb5-8378-1f535c4f0420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455199} :answer-field/id #uuid "866e10cb-7bc8-54b5-b98f-2a2d55ebe453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a3675b0-e883-52dd-8e3e-7330cc6b3856" :question/math-academy-id "q-165415" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e845a04-8cdb-5e9e-84f7-e0e83c51ac51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455073} {:answer/id #uuid "e5794dc8-ca22-54fc-a518-c6c715487e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455300} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a093ac-9f78-5544-b061-1933a24a44b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455305} {:answer/id #uuid "f895f8f8-5c23-5c95-9255-aeebe933932a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f895f8f8-5c23-5c95-9255-aeebe933932a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455330} :answer-field/id #uuid "8751bc0c-cc97-539c-aaed-dee2b1d55e70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86bb2466-6029-5b95-bd68-985d08738376" :question/math-academy-id "q-344258" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fa23ce-2a64-5f21-af68-65dc34a26e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454978} {:answer/id #uuid "966b9893-02ce-5002-92b6-528d0cbf1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455174} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4cbb92-f7b5-5cd0-b586-5f6f713d2615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455258} {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea66f81-9d52-5528-86a7-49bf7d5e5e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0337040-d506-5af7-a704-b40619be2791" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a34abd9-b695-5ab7-980c-61ca9112523d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344326" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+10y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+10y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+B\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{10}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{10}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=6$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 6 & =-A\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{10})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{10}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a75b67-5f2f-522b-91db-f97635ae5971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454937} {:answer/id #uuid "2405db00-e007-59b1-bf44-c16b5a7629f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454958} {:answer/id #uuid "8a580176-26a2-5536-906f-5573392c1d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455155} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c54752-bf5b-5114-8706-a39bfe9f6e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455279} {:answer/id #uuid "e81cddbe-cf94-5088-b2c6-2672b342f18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a580176-26a2-5536-906f-5573392c1d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455155} :answer-field/id #uuid "d82fe19b-8cd0-5df1-bf94-5bdbb9c5b9df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c7ce8e8-7ebb-5185-86d9-47c695c84f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-76768" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ because $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}⟹\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =32{\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63827e74-514c-5021-80bc-37896fead1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455084} {:answer/id #uuid "7614d0d6-6711-55f7-ae39-6d253da5157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455119} {:answer/id #uuid "995b2231-16fd-5d6a-88df-4e7b9bc77275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455182} {:answer/id #uuid "e26b28e3-ff13-5e5c-b422-64465c751cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26b28e3-ff13-5e5c-b422-64465c751cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47b3730-54e2-5abe-9f7e-75ee2c91ff0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae30a8eb-a3b6-5b76-8879-c28474760fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-344284" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-5,5]$, where $f$ is $10$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $10$-periodic on $[-5,5]$, we have $L=5$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc074f0-8d82-55d9-960a-7332cb2a9aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454931} {:answer/id #uuid "1a14529d-3768-5610-b079-b36a0abcae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454945} {:answer/id #uuid "3dabd53e-5bb6-5035-b84f-4f492fd16bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455006} {:answer/id #uuid "705ccca2-e01f-5eb0-be9c-6619697c9f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455112} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fc3cdf-cbc6-5629-a612-73348098ca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a14529d-3768-5610-b079-b36a0abcae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454945} :answer-field/id #uuid "30132071-c6bc-5d04-908b-c84e3cfc46ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcf9f8c6-5d3c-5211-90e2-bddb94921272" :question/math-academy-id "q-76609" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d8db51-d8e5-5c78-97de-cf185417daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454976} {:answer/id #uuid "985f359f-8702-569a-bb17-1736d6a27b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455180} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdf9269-0054-577d-9b4b-61ffbea6989f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455186} {:answer/id #uuid "d1181996-5194-59b4-966f-48575fc3d5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985f359f-8702-569a-bb17-1736d6a27b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455180} :answer-field/id #uuid "c62d441d-cf47-52e7-b38c-fb09c667ff01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc163f49-3433-502e-a178-2f1ecb1f8055" :question/math-academy-id "q-344038" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+3{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\pi \\,\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{3}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{3}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{3}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{3}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(12-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42db5338-1e39-52a9-9b3f-1945f45b8bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455015} {:answer/id #uuid "66a47cde-b229-5742-a3c9-cd9a46d82b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455091} {:answer/id #uuid "8942f4a7-3e21-56af-9bb4-658bf93f6604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455151} {:answer/id #uuid "ad686914-2f26-5181-bfe3-df5b8db65ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455207} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea4357-cdc0-5451-a938-bd13cfa04e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8942f4a7-3e21-56af-9bb4-658bf93f6604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455151} :answer-field/id #uuid "300a6c45-5c56-5b70-8220-7a36e97b7d82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce434154-3398-5488-977f-b3adb13e5b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-76607" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082112eb-8a26-5265-b137-26bd8c295b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454924} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae41ca2-e608-5696-b440-d41c065df75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454928} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbc00c8-62ae-5a6f-8037-f55f4dd09a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455070} {:answer/id #uuid "76d63731-dd9b-510e-8131-e402a45ba6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455120} {:answer/id #uuid "de572935-367e-52cb-8f52-f59649494fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cbc00c8-62ae-5a6f-8037-f55f4dd09a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455070} :answer-field/id #uuid "436cc0aa-bff3-5b92-a639-ba2e62b0da93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee634955-812e-57a4-9691-da172190ae0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76611" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e86b23-ab58-5191-ad26-f69ae3689883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454977} {:answer/id #uuid "436fc72b-b372-56c4-8b22-e7ad0b501850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455016} {:answer/id #uuid "669bf703-838e-5938-ae0d-38fb979f2c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455090} {:answer/id #uuid "79e64d1a-8928-56a0-b539-e3221053a6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455127} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8a62c2-9594-5471-887e-0b239c4e7aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669bf703-838e-5938-ae0d-38fb979f2c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0754811-61f8-5b43-b64d-d1916d6e8f79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eec86b54-2bc2-5f46-8f9a-0f8c56be4962" :question/math-academy-id "q-76769" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =27{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}27{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =81\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}((\\frac{1}{2})^{6}-{0}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a56a8231-fa23-5f97-b089-27fbbd678426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a56a8231-fa23-5f97-b089-27fbbd678426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458921} :answer-field/id #uuid "a13a9d5d-2416-5caf-aa6b-9a5345f1752d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00c8c3c6-017f-53bd-8aef-13e6a3aec175" :question/math-academy-id "q-171540" :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{2}q-\\frac{7}{6}q=${{field-1}}\n$q$" :question/worked-solution "We combine the like terms by subtracting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}q-\\frac{7}{6}q &= (\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{6})q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the coefficients, we have to convert them to have the same denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{6})q &= (\\frac{15}{6}-\\frac{7}{6})q \\\\ &= (\\frac{15-7}{6})q \\\\ &= \\frac{8}{6}q \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{3}\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19813603-2ff6-5970-bfa0-a18fe649a92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458670} {:answer/id #uuid "2e952626-46ee-5082-8870-a4f12c88b01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458719} {:answer/id #uuid "6224b75f-2ecf-55a0-8d31-a1c8dd965483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458797} {:answer/id #uuid "b79d8989-3956-514d-8613-e3f7d9d32e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{8}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458960} {:answer/id #uuid "ff65897b-ae3f-5dde-b154-c5eaeea76e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19813603-2ff6-5970-bfa0-a18fe649a92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458670} :answer-field/id #uuid "a52eccb8-b500-5312-ae2a-22b1fe553ea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "020fed93-fcd3-5223-ae8a-0e7f2cc99320" :question/math-academy-id "q-51440" :question/problem "$\\frac{y}{8}+7-\\frac{3y}{8}-11=$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{8}+7-\\frac{3y}{8}-11 &= (\\frac{y}{8}-\\frac{3y}{8})+(7-11) \\\\ &= (\\frac{y-3y}{8})-4 \\\\ &= -\\frac{2y}{8}-4 \\\\ &= -\\frac{y}{4}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36dea13-9c1f-5885-939b-78c3be24015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b36dea13-9c1f-5885-939b-78c3be24015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458952} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a35d36-00f8-577a-8dd4-9fb713b96b96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ef347b8-64ff-5465-9d5f-8f167a76e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-316298" :question/problem "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{6}{\\pi }(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}))-(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot 0)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0+0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }[(\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }(\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244c1d8b-d2e8-5d24-8f75-383e240735c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244c1d8b-d2e8-5d24-8f75-383e240735c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458700} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8b7040b-1c28-5651-827d-d9a23db3b9d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "280ecae1-be4d-57a7-89a2-e0f1aff5f58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344694" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079e330a-ed7c-5585-893b-009f113e791f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}m+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458629} {:answer/id #uuid "6202db53-1c31-5b08-a010-38ef6126b872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}m+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458795} {:answer/id #uuid "6481e2e7-aaff-5589-acf0-e3b8ef29ba0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458800} {:answer/id #uuid "652bbb0b-a61f-5ea0-8bdc-a045d8ae7042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}m-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458805} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3d776-8cef-5da7-94a2-8514d7604576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}m+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3d776-8cef-5da7-94a2-8514d7604576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}m+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458848} :answer-field/id #uuid "267a169a-8097-5d51-bee3-87afdd83ccc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37caea09-08c3-57fc-903e-0d9f1b5634da" :question/math-academy-id "q-51439" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{5}m+8-\\frac{6}{5}m-3=$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}m+8-\\frac{6}{5}m-3 &= (\\frac{2}{5}m-\\frac{6}{5}m)+(8-3) \\\\ &= (\\frac{2-6}{5})m+5 \\\\ &= -\\frac{4}{5}m+5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c63519-b99a-535f-a767-aba13aa8c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458623} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea786b-6aeb-5ab2-a668-e87a4f9adbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458680} {:answer/id #uuid "279f798e-fca6-5883-b6f2-651083c8d7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458707} {:answer/id #uuid "72c2755d-f02b-5787-8c1c-3f4bebe47eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458827} {:answer/id #uuid "e75890bc-ed87-53a8-8ce4-3a04a435feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279f798e-fca6-5883-b6f2-651083c8d7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12f6dde-e5b0-5b0d-8795-17186369244e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "381f7568-7449-53f3-9fba-a89117d8269e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37235" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{6}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{21}{5} & \\frac{42}{5} & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$  Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ \nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=1+1=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769c9f8d-44e3-5cb7-8aa3-d35e0ebe3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "769c9f8d-44e3-5cb7-8aa3-d35e0ebe3590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458836} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e6d683-4798-50e1-8613-685a14130585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fedff19-9172-5d54-8787-5512405d7cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fedff19-9172-5d54-8787-5512405d7cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458688} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a92f75-9f75-5e24-9502-2166440337ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "382c9a42-23fb-50da-958e-822e7f1e9af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-178171" :question/problem "$2a+8(\\frac{5a}{2}-\\frac{b}{4})=${{field-1}}\n$a\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$b$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the distributive law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+8(\\frac{5a}{2}-\\frac{b}{4}) &= 2a+8(\\frac{5a}{2})-8(\\frac{b}{4}) \\\\ &= 2a+\\frac{40a}{2}-\\frac{8b}{4} \\\\ &= 2a+20a-2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+20a-2b &= (2a+20a)-2b \\\\ &= 22\\,a-2\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0462c5a-e194-5968-aa3c-30b604665076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0462c5a-e194-5968-aa3c-30b604665076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ca3de3-8094-51d4-be18-d519acf04d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2fa558e-45d2-5d75-8de8-3dcedde3e30f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344550" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ on the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-a)f(c) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{1-0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{1}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1))-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1))] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{c+1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ c+1 & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c09e5b-7e4c-5782-8384-76280dc7cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23x}{35}+22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458618} {:answer/id #uuid "01c5e166-86c7-5683-922c-c6a72cf2a69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22x}{35}+23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458619} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca191ae-ecd6-5959-8171-b55afa13cc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35x}{23}-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458744} {:answer/id #uuid "a539b382-351d-5f45-96fd-7219ec78e7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23x}{35}-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458920} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f90038-622c-52d6-84df-3ba78748cf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22x}{5}+23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c5e166-86c7-5683-922c-c6a72cf2a69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22x}{35}+23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458619} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa284a81-c607-55d4-8e5d-f033a3ac3d07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc80fc0c-88ad-5f7a-b174-a89a65c11a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-100054" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{6x}{5}+15-\\frac{4x}{7}+8.$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x}{5}+15-\\frac{4x}{7}+8 &= (\\frac{6x}{5}-\\frac{4x}{7})+(15+8) \\\\ &= (\\frac{42x}{35}-\\frac{20x}{35})+(15+8) \\\\ &= (\\frac{42x-20x}{35})+23 \\\\ &= \\frac{22x}{35}+23\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0208fff1-48ef-55ae-918c-33a68cb210b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475448} {:answer/id #uuid "1b862eea-1214-550a-ac2e-86a12ab5cf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475479} {:answer/id #uuid "40fd6d88-af50-539b-972c-9ee1801ae51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475538} {:answer/id #uuid "b30f8ce3-eff6-5c08-9a37-7caca188a6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475707} {:answer/id #uuid "f036051f-f9c0-5d70-a495-24cb170dde51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0208fff1-48ef-55ae-918c-33a68cb210b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475448} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b99618-5fde-5a9c-8c62-230bd72eace2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "050071aa-cca7-508c-9680-385a0e8970cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-104595" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{0}=$" :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{7}{10})}^{0}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae753e7-70e5-5221-84b1-e330ef9c348d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ae753e7-70e5-5221-84b1-e330ef9c348d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475651} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b88d6e-de4a-5887-8e9c-fdbdf319a532" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f1f731d-e9c5-5dff-95c0-76702cc6de15" :question/math-academy-id "q-171386" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{1}{1,000}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $1$ and $1000$ are both perfect cubes.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{1}{1,000}},$ we express $-\\frac{1}{1,000}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{1}{1,000}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{{10}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-1)}{10\\cdot 10\\cdot 10}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{1}{10})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{10})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{10})} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{1}{10})}^{3}} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "612f3cb8-e5e7-5d6e-b940-51d288212baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "612f3cb8-e5e7-5d6e-b940-51d288212baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475586} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d432477-4db5-5583-ac69-893afb3defd6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10213554-6b50-5566-8a12-87c52f6a8607" :question/math-academy-id "q-186159" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{64}{27}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $64$ and $27$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{64}{27}},$ we express $\\frac{64}{27}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{64}{27}} &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ddc08d-d22f-5193-bf06-38fb8c7ba7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475455} {:answer/id #uuid "0af3f2c2-59c9-5249-a2d8-811877a6ca18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475461} {:answer/id #uuid "19987fb0-bc40-51ba-b84d-d8cb0087f00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475476} {:answer/id #uuid "226d0641-c568-50b8-93be-58d7db8760ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475487} {:answer/id #uuid "87b7e1f6-b074-5206-8b9b-5827ab7b73bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0af3f2c2-59c9-5249-a2d8-811877a6ca18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475461} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ad6879-6146-512a-b9ce-7b997929ffe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "480ef21f-535f-5e73-9903-6cc50742dda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-102713" :question/problem "Consider the surface $z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz=2+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)k \\\\  & =(-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-yz)i+(-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-xz)j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xy)k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }i+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\pi \\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+(1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,\\,-\\pi ,\\,1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\pi ,\\,-\\pi ,\\,\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4}{4}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\pi }{-\\pi }=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295d7cb2-c41d-5562-9627-6496a886b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475497} {:answer/id #uuid "3d55582d-c167-5af8-9b20-b189409d9555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{12}{24})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475528} {:answer/id #uuid "8e02d7ce-4d35-5d10-8941-b9e49476be78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475657} {:answer/id #uuid "ea43961e-5665-55b5-af85-109b62d090bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{9}{64})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475803} {:answer/id #uuid "f5295237-0532-5388-98f4-1f1ab70a871a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5295237-0532-5388-98f4-1f1ab70a871a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5872048b-a786-5fc6-96af-eb26baac894e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6455f903-95ba-56a7-8ef0-339948a725c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75533" :question/problem "$(-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})=$" :question/worked-solution "The number $(-\\frac{3}{8})$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})\\times (-\\frac{3}{8})}_{4\\text{ copies of }(-\\frac{3}{8})}={(-\\frac{3}{8})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is ${(-\\frac{3}{8})}^{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b244d36-8d67-5320-9035-8dd827bd0705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475556} {:answer/id #uuid "61cdfc2f-1158-5a15-b1f1-37c9d98ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{80}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475588} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b3bed6-f0c1-5c82-8a64-504415722091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475689} {:answer/id #uuid "f6111da0-a076-5189-8ba0-6669dae210ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475819} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbbc3dd-1d58-5aea-a5d7-9d57161c9bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6111da0-a076-5189-8ba0-6669dae210ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475819} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9ebbd7-feaa-584e-bd9d-c167a2ceed32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8735f04-f98b-594f-8e92-cc8bc0a03443" :question/math-academy-id "q-100194" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{80}{49})}^{0}=$" :questi